### What is contract-first API development?

The OpenAPI document is a real artifact in version control, reviewed like code, and exists before any implementation.

```yaml
# transfer-api.yaml - the single source of truth
openapi: 3.0.3
info:
  title: Transfer Service API
  version: 1.0.0
paths:
  /api/v1/transfers:
    post:
      summary: Initiate a transfer
      requestBody:
        required: true
        content:
          application/json:
            schema:
              $ref: '#/components/schemas/TransferRequest'
      responses:
        '201':
          description: Transfer initiated
          content:
            application/json:
              schema:
                $ref: '#/components/schemas/TransferResponse'
components:
  schemas:
    TransferRequest:
      type: object
      required: [fromAccountId, toAccountId, amount]
      properties:
        fromAccountId:
          type: string
        toAccountId:
          type: string
        amount:
          type: number
          format: double
    TransferResponse:
      type: object
      properties:
        transferId:
          type: string
        status:
          type: string
```

```text
Contract-first workflow:
  1. Teams agree on the OpenAPI spec
  2. Generate server stubs + client SDKs
  3. Backend implements the generated interfaces
  4. Frontend builds against generated client or mock
  5. Diff the spec in CI to catch breaking changes
```

### How does code generation eliminate drift?

Both server and client are generated from the same spec. If the spec changes, regeneration immediately surfaces compile errors.

```java
package com.bank.api;

// Generated interface - developer implements this
public interface TransferApi {
    TransferResponse createTransfer(TransferRequest request);
}

// Developer's implementation
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferApiImpl implements TransferApi {
    @Override
    public TransferResponse createTransfer(TransferRequest request) {
        // Business logic here
        return new TransferResponse("TXN-001", "INITIATED");
    }
}
```

```text
If spec removes 'status' from TransferResponse:
  1. Regenerate -> TransferResponse no longer has 'status'
  2. TransferApiImpl: compile error where it sets 'status'
  3. Developer must fix before commit
  4. Drift eliminated BY CONSTRUCTION
```

### How does spec diffing catch breaking changes in CI?

A diff tool compares the new spec to the baseline and classifies changes as compatible or breaking.

```java
package com.bank.api;

public class SpecDiffing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CI spec diff results:");
        System.out.println("  COMPATIBLE: added optional field 'description' to TransferRequest");
        System.out.println("  COMPATIBLE: added new endpoint GET /transfers/{id}");
        System.out.println("  BREAKING: removed field 'status' from TransferResponse -> BUILD FAILS");
        System.out.println("  BREAKING: changed 'amount' type from number to string -> BUILD FAILS");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Breaking changes caught BEFORE code is written");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
CI spec diff results:
  COMPATIBLE: added optional field 'description' to TransferRequest
  COMPATIBLE: added new endpoint GET /transfers/{id}
  BREAKING: removed field 'status' from TransferResponse -> BUILD FAILS
  BREAKING: changed 'amount' type from number to string -> BUILD FAILS

Breaking changes caught BEFORE code is written
```

### How does code-first differ?

You write controllers and the spec is derived from annotations. The contract is implicit and can change by accident.

```java
package com.bank.api;

public class CodeFirstProblem {
    // Code-first: developer renames a field during refactoring
    // Before: public String holderName;
    // After:  public String customerName;
    // springdoc regenerates spec -> field silently renamed
    // Mobile team's client breaks at runtime
    // Nobody noticed until production!

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Code-first weakness:");
        System.out.println("  Developer renames holderName -> customerName");
        System.out.println("  Spec auto-generated -> field renamed silently");
        System.out.println("  No CI check caught it");
        System.out.println("  Mobile app breaks in production");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Contract-first solution:");
        System.out.println("  Spec is the source of truth");
        System.out.println("  CI diff would have caught the rename as BREAKING");
        System.out.println("  Build would have FAILED before deployment");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Code-first weakness:
  Developer renames holderName -> customerName
  Spec auto-generated -> field renamed silently
  Mobile app breaks in production

Contract-first solution:
  CI diff would have caught the rename as BREAKING
  Build would have FAILED before deployment
```

### How does parallel development work?

Frontend generates a client and builds against a mock server. Backend implements the generated interfaces. Both work from the same spec.

```java
package com.bank.api;

public class ParallelDevelopment {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Parallel development with contract-first:");
        System.out.println("  Day 1: Teams agree on OpenAPI spec");
        System.out.println("  Day 2-5:");
        System.out.println("    Mobile team: generates client SDK, codes against mock server");
        System.out.println("    Backend team: implements generated server interfaces");
        System.out.println("    Partner team: generates their client SDK");
        System.out.println("  Day 6: Both sides connect -> works because same spec");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Without contract-first:");
        System.out.println("  Mobile team waits for backend to finish");
        System.out.println("  Then discovers the API shape is different from what they assumed");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Parallel development with contract-first:
  Day 1: Teams agree on OpenAPI spec
  Day 2-5: All teams build in parallel from same spec
  Day 6: Both sides connect -> works
```

### When do you use code-first vs contract-first?

| Scenario | Approach | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Public or cross-team API | Contract-first | Many consumers, stability critical |
| Internal service, one team, rapid change | Code-first | Convenience, few consumers |
| Partner integration | Contract-first | Contractual stability required |
| Prototype or MVP | Code-first | Speed over stability |

```java
package com.bank.api;

public class WhenToUseWhich {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Contract-first for:");
        System.out.println("  Public APIs, cross-team APIs, partner integrations");
        System.out.println("  Where breaking changes cost real money");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Code-first for:");
        System.out.println("  Small internal services, single team, rapid iteration");
        System.out.println("  Where the contract is simple and consumers are few");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Middle ground: code-first with spec committed and diffed in CI");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Contract-first for: public/cross-team APIs, partner integrations
Code-first for: small internal services, rapid iteration
Middle ground: code-first with spec committed and diffed in CI
```

### What is the pragmatic middle ground?

Code-first with the generated spec committed to version control and diffed in CI gives some guardrails without full spec authoring.

```java
package com.bank.api;

public class MiddleGround {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pragmatic middle ground:");
        System.out.println("  1. Write controllers normally (code-first)");
        System.out.println("  2. springdoc generates OpenAPI spec");
        System.out.println("  3. Commit the generated spec to version control");
        System.out.println("  4. CI diffs the spec against the previous version");
        System.out.println("  5. Breaking changes still fail the build");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Less ceremony than full contract-first");
        System.out.println("  More safety than pure code-first");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Pragmatic middle ground:
  1. Write controllers (code-first)
  2. Generate spec
  3. Commit spec
  4. Diff in CI
  5. Breaking changes still caught
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The real goal is that breaking changes require a deliberate, reviewed decision rather than slipping through as an ordinary code edit.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Contract-first for public/cross-team APIs
  [x] Code-first with spec diffing for internal APIs
  [x] openapi-generator for server stubs and client SDKs
  [x] Spec diff in CI: adding optional = pass, removing = fail
  [x] Version the API only when absolutely necessary
  [x] The goal: breaking changes are deliberate decisions, not accidents
```
